rdlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/plain; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn33view0\",\"pattern\":\"v\"}); Total lines: 253\nL192: ## Running GEAK on the codex CLI\nL193: \nL194: GEAK also runs on the **codex CLI**, with the same `.js` workflows unmodified.\nL195: \nL196: ### 1. Install\nL197: \nL198: ```bash\nL199: node -v                            # need Node.js v20+\nL200: npm i -g @openai/codex@0.146.1     # pin 0.146.1 -- 0.147 breaks with gateways\nL201: codex --version                    # expect 0.146.1\nL202: node interface/runtime/engine/selftest.mjs  # optional: runtime checks, needs no GPU and no key\nL203: ```\nL204: ### 2. Configure\nL205: \nL206: One variable is the whole configuration: it **selects codex** *and* **configures its provider**.\nL207: \nL208: ```bash\nL209: export OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-...        # -> OpenAI official (api.openai.com)\nL210: # export GEAK_AMDKEY=<32hex>        # -> AMD gateway (llm-api.amd.com/Unified)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCAKE from Scratch: Agents Write GPU Kernels, and the Compiler Learns from Their Mistakes | Gang Liao (https://gangliao.me/blog/2026/cake-deep-dive/)\nciteturn37view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn35view3\",\"pattern\":\"author\"}); Total lines: 718\nL188: The author of this blog is the first author of KernelEvolve, which the CAKE paper cites as an example of evolutionary search over kernel candidates. We describe it, like every other system here, only as far as CAKE's argument needs, and we use the paper's characterization.\nL189: ### 5.2 What the environment says back\nL190: \nL191: This loop works well for local tuning: try a different tile size, unroll a loop, fuse two operations. It struggles with the decisions from §4, for a reason the paper puts in one sentence: compiler errors, correctness outcomes, and end-to-end timing “never say which program decision caused a synchronization failure, a hardware-contract violation, or a pipeline stall.”\nL192: \nL193: Think about what each signal tells an agent that just rewrote a pipelined kernel:\nL294: A good interface for a fallible author, human or AI, separates the choices that matter (which role, how many stages, which memory) from the consequences that follow mechanically (addresses, phase bits, encodings). The first kind should be written down and checked; the second should never be written by hand at all.\nL298: The §7.1 fragment already contains two such commitments: `pool.view(offset=0, ...)` says the ring starts at byte 0 of the pool, and `lm.tmem(cols=0, width=128, ...)` says the accumulator occupies tensor-memory columns 0 to 127. A swizzle is a fixed shuffling of where elements sit in shared memory, so that threads reading together do not collide on the same memory bank.\nL299: If, say, the TMA copy wrote a tile with one swizzle and the MMA read it assuming another, the compiler would point at the disagreeing declaration before anything runs. (The example is ours; the paper describes the checks only by category.)\nL300: This is the reverse of CuTe’s bargain. CuTe asks the author to be an expert in layouts and rewards them with expressive power. CAKE asks the author for plain facts and makes the compiler responsible for catching contradictions. The paper describes its layout verifier only by its contract and coverage, not its internals.\nL301: ### 7.4 One language, many GPUs\nL302: The same schedule language targets NVIDIA GPUs from Ampere through Blackwell. The structure of a role–barrier–pipeline schedule carries over; which instructions are allowed, and how they are lowered, remains specific to each target.\nL303: (The `sm_` names are NVIDIA’s codes for GPU generations: `sm_80` is Ampere, `sm_90a` Hopper, `sm_100a` Blackwell; the trailing a marks features specific to that chip.) You do not need the details below; the right-hand column just shows the instruction set changing under the same schedule structure.\nL701:   * cite16†KernelBench (arXiv:2502.10517)†arxiv.org : the benchmark that set up the “PyTorch operator in, fast kernel out” task.\nL702:   * cite21†KernelEvolve (arXiv:2512.23236)†arxiv.org : agentic kernel coding for heterogeneous accelerators at Meta (the author of this blog is a co-author).\nL703:   * cite60†AlphaEvolve†arxiv.org and cite61†FunSearch (Nature, 2023)†www.nature.com : LLM-driven evolutionary program search.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nForgeStencil: Automating Per-Case Stencil Specialization from Kernels to 100+ Real Applications (https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06694v2)\nciteturn37view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn18view1\",\"pattern\":\"tokens\"}); Total lines: 489\nNo matching text found for \"tokens\""
